#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059382 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059823 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb626c84-db31-5e1b-90cb-3f5d13ee0af5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals From Large Samples"}] :question/id #uuid "97c8f1dc-747e-5aa4-ab4f-eb809abb560e" :question/math-academy-id "e-10035" :question/problem "Consider a sample of size $n=196$  from a population with standard deviation $\\sigma=7.$ Given that the mean of the sample is $\\overline{x}=24,$ find a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z > 1.96) = 0.025,$ where $Z \\sim N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram below.*\n\n![](images/28/28f56656e191e342a4815ba74bdd29cba5247fba7d8ab0feca8ffbe0b785ab8c.png)" :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size $n = 196 \\geq 30$ is *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\mu}{\\sigma/\\sqrt n} \\sim N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overline{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean,\n\n- $\\sigma^2$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} \\pm z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $Z \\sim N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.95=0.05 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z > 1.96) = 0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\nz_{\\alpha/2} = z_{0.025} = 1.96.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24\\pm 1.96\\cdot \\frac{7}{\\sqrt{196}} \\\\ 24\\pm 0.98\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059383 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059824 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "27d20d28-3fba-5830-8bff-76e9a1c24bec" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals: Applications"}] :question/id #uuid "3189a42a-200e-5caa-bc76-6ac1f4ae424d" :question/math-academy-id "e-10039" :question/problem "The daily percentage return of a particular stock has a population standard deviation of $\\sigma = 5\\%.$ A sample of $90$ randomly selected trading days was examined, and it was found that the mean daily return of the stock over these days was $7.5\\%.$ Find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the mean daily percentage return $\\mu$ of the entire population of trading days.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z \\sim N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores $z_p$ such that $P(Z > z_p) = p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $z_p$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size $n = 90\\geq 30$ is *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\mu}{\\sigma/\\sqrt n} \\sim N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overline{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean,\n\n- $\\sigma^2$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} \\pm z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $Z \\sim N(0,1).$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overline{x}=7.5, \\qquad\nn = 90, \\qquad\n\\sigma = 5.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.9=0.1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value $z_{0.05}$ such that $P(Z > z_{0.05}) = 0.05,$ where $Z \\sim N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/2f/2fc6327df99c6b88508b0c4de2e4edf6c5521974f05e2e2f02d09786937285a7.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that $z_{0.05}=1.645{:}$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.050}$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $z_p$ | $1.282$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1.645}$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, the $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7.5\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{90}} \\\\ 7.5\\pm 0.867\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059384 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059825 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b90997d0-b563-5541-9fe2-36d7457da7b5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals From Normal Populations"}] :question/id #uuid "3a166c80-cc70-5dd3-9dbe-2798dc882199" :question/math-academy-id "e-10046" :question/problem "Consider a sample of size $n=9$ from a normal population with standard deviation $\\sigma=2.$ Given that the mean of the sample is $\\overline{x}=-5,$ find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z > 1.645) = 0.05,$ where $Z \\sim N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram below.*\n\n![](images/39/39a83b1081d22919c9549c4ff1c6ad8006dc802cc23f9802d762e97883bea48c.png)" :question/worked-solution "We are told that the population is normally distributed. Therefore, the distribution of sample mean is also normal, and we have that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\mu}{\\sigma/\\sqrt n} \\sim N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overline{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu$ is the population mean,\n\n- $\\sigma^2$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} \\pm z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $Z \\sim N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.9=0.1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z > 1.645) = 0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\nz_{\\alpha/2} = z_{0.05} = 1.645.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\ -5\\pm 1.097\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060050 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059823 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb626c84-db31-5e1b-90cb-3f5d13ee0af5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals From Large Samples"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d616e9b-b822-52b9-a10e-4189b937a1be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14\\pm 0.98" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497829} {:answer/id #uuid "29796b67-7bcc-55f0-9517-3abd2cef4b6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25\\pm 1.96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497884} {:answer/id #uuid "67d46b32-db24-5401-952b-f0869939e48c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25\\pm 0.98" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498049} {:answer/id #uuid "7691218c-ed40-5f8c-9d71-ed78baa0649c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14\\pm 2.56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498081} {:answer/id #uuid "a11f5764-4f74-5c87-95e2-5a3a414d074a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14\\pm 1.96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498160}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d616e9b-b822-52b9-a10e-4189b937a1be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14\\pm 0.98" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497829} :answer-field/id #uuid "f12506e4-c772-5058-86a7-947724feb700" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056658}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "36cd39e3-b51f-590a-80b3-45da7f899cac" :question/math-academy-id "q-142303" :question/problem "![](images/28/28f56656e191e342a4815ba74bdd29cba5247fba7d8ab0feca8ffbe0b785ab8c.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=100$  from a population with standard deviation $\\sigma =5.$ Given that the mean of the sample is $\\overset{―}{x}=14,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size $n=100\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.025}=1.96.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14\\pm 1.96\\cdot \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{100}} \\\\ 14\\pm 0.98\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498533 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059823 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb626c84-db31-5e1b-90cb-3f5d13ee0af5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals From Large Samples"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3fb55e05-078b-5bd9-bee9-11b34b25c2ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pm 0.438" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497942} {:answer/id #uuid "5245fbae-682c-5200-a3cc-b240ba6e18a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pm 0.490" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497995} {:answer/id #uuid "8ac017e5-f420-5574-9450-e76853f09d22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pm 0.392" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498118} {:answer/id #uuid "9f618acf-fb41-51a6-99f4-2df360eb2078" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80\\pm 4.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498156} {:answer/id #uuid "bfe37f3a-324e-5cb5-84ab-a56302e24787" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pm 0.859" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498226}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3fb55e05-078b-5bd9-bee9-11b34b25c2ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pm 0.438" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497942} :answer-field/id #uuid "013e8c02-58d5-5e41-b09e-a87d19761e14" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498361}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4c371072-128a-5610-adfb-0397399d02d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-142491" :question/problem "![](images/28/28f56656e191e342a4815ba74bdd29cba5247fba7d8ab0feca8ffbe0b785ab8c.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=80$  from a population with standard deviation $\\sigma =2.$ Given that the mean of the sample is $\\overset{―}{x}=-2,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size $n=80\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.025}=1.96.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\pm 1.96\\cdot \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{80}} \\\\ -2\\pm 0.438\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498542 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059823 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb626c84-db31-5e1b-90cb-3f5d13ee0af5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals From Large Samples"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26f43a97-858d-5d6b-8e14-2ca1af1707b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pm 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497877} {:answer/id #uuid "419aefd4-69a2-580b-b986-0da3f154ef5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "135\\pm 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497947} {:answer/id #uuid "4d69db6f-7ccc-5702-a556-c06d016e9316" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pm 1.645" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497980} {:answer/id #uuid "4e998fe4-61fb-520e-8733-db8847569f9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "135\\pm 3.29" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497986} {:answer/id #uuid "74c43959-d0c0-50b0-94f0-843272fe1211" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pm 0.142" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498078}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74c43959-d0c0-50b0-94f0-843272fe1211" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pm 0.142" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498078} :answer-field/id #uuid "77f21f06-2475-57bd-a2cb-3044a83d5e18" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498417}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "64f21f0a-233c-5960-8d23-c7d29d8f3ed3" :question/math-academy-id "q-142497" :question/problem "![](images/39/39a83b1081d22919c9549c4ff1c6ad8006dc802cc23f9802d762e97883bea48c.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=135$  from a population with standard deviation $\\sigma =1.$ Given that the mean of the sample is $\\overset{―}{x}=-2,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size $n=135\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.90=0.10\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.05}=1.645.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{135}} \\\\ -2\\pm 0.142\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498559 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059823 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb626c84-db31-5e1b-90cb-3f5d13ee0af5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals From Large Samples"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00d5769c-b5ff-5fe6-bfd4-f75f9a168407" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14.4\\pm 2.576" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497799} {:answer/id #uuid "16f87830-e8d8-51ae-82fa-53af8da47616" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\pm 0.425" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497853} {:answer/id #uuid "4a605826-710d-5524-b97d-c06b411fd2fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\pm 0.713" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497969} {:answer/id #uuid "5fecd59c-92b6-5dd8-9e61-56150279d05d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\pm 0.718" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498026} {:answer/id #uuid "6f5dcaf7-c693-5f07-83ec-e1867e324afc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\pm 0.429" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498068}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f5dcaf7-c693-5f07-83ec-e1867e324afc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\pm 0.429" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498068} :answer-field/id #uuid "4fab1b25-a070-5368-a780-857e62a9b5a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498393}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "87f55a60-f84e-5cb1-9665-2c11dc9713b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-142500" :question/problem "![](images/64/6486d3914bb0f7b4898220512e96630d13d2b0f4a5c1851938f98371a4c0255f.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=144$  from a population with standard deviation $\\sigma =2.$ Given that the mean of the sample is $\\overset{―}{x}=10,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size $n=144\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.005}=2.576.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10\\pm 2.576\\cdot \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{144}} \\\\ 10\\pm 0.429\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498580 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059823 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb626c84-db31-5e1b-90cb-3f5d13ee0af5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals From Large Samples"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c7c535d-0d9a-5a44-a538-4f046c0f3916" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12\\pm 1.748" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497826} {:answer/id #uuid "3afa5f38-b925-516d-80ee-5acdd056b9f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12\\pm 0.687" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497933} {:answer/id #uuid "3d18e529-adf9-5b89-94e9-0fde27f40cb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12\\pm 0.183" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497937} {:answer/id #uuid "6a724e2c-cb0b-5c9c-adcf-1e12eb4