an $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.005}=2.576.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-12\\pm 2.576\\cdot \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{225}} \\\\ -12\\pm 0.687\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498518 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059825 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b90997d0-b563-5541-9fe2-36d7457da7b5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals From Normal Populations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01f96de5-dbdf-5045-bfec-c96bf5b0e350" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22\\pm 0.823" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497802} {:answer/id #uuid "18db88ac-9193-5677-bce3-d86fd6469a6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19\\pm 1.156" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497857} {:answer/id #uuid "2bc03372-7346-5af4-aa8f-947357c87bc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\pm 1.123" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497889} {:answer/id #uuid "355a5122-c31e-5d74-bdf3-1044eb577532" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22\\pm 0.98" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497918} {:answer/id #uuid "fa43971d-f124-5992-a95a-4b9b70ecf9b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22\\pm 1.252" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498347}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01f96de5-dbdf-5045-bfec-c96bf5b0e350" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22\\pm 0.823" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497802} :answer-field/id #uuid "f832b1d1-a2f7-5a16-9ffd-1dcb274b8fae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498483}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3c88652f-90de-5af1-9dba-66fecdb99433" :question/math-academy-id "q-142523" :question/problem "![](images/39/39a83b1081d22919c9549c4ff1c6ad8006dc802cc23f9802d762e97883bea48c.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=16$ from a normal population with standard deviation $\\sigma =2.$ Given that the mean of the sample is $\\overset{―}{x}=22,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" :question/worked-solution "We are told that the population is normally distributed. Therefore, the distribution of sample mean is also normal, and we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.05}=1.645.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}22\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{16}} \\\\ 22\\pm 0.823\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060054 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059824 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "27d20d28-3fba-5830-8bff-76e9a1c24bec" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals: Applications"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03590c25-5754-5f22-a19f-227f931d9ca2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100\\pm 2.576\\,\\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497806} {:answer/id #uuid "31773c72-42fa-5a91-a142-e52eda53327f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\pm 2.576\\,\\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497902} {:answer/id #uuid "336113ef-d7ab-5192-b8d5-39d93c496600" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\pm 9.775\\,\\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497908} {:answer/id #uuid "b5523cdb-710f-5180-88b7-c913d39f57ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100\\pm 12.271\\,\\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498205} {:answer/id #uuid "f6bc7b88-7472-5702-92e6-9883d0bec8bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100\\pm 9.775\\,\\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498339}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6bc7b88-7472-5702-92e6-9883d0bec8bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100\\pm 9.775\\,\\text{g}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498339} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d59fd8c-4f21-517e-93e3-05cac2fbde83" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186056682}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d5864ffb-fe58-5217-afdf-eabb7e2ece4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-142535" :question/problem "The weight of apples in a particular harvest is normally distributed with standard deviation $\\sigma =12\\,\\text{g}.$ A farmer sampled $10$ apples from the harvest and found that their mean weight is $100\\,\\text{g}.$ Find a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean weight $\\mu $ for the entire harvest. \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "We are told that the population is normally distributed. Therefore, the distribution of sample mean is also normal, and we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=100\\,\\text{g},\\,n=10,\\,\\sigma =12\\,\\text{g}.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.005}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.005})=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/8c/8c5ac93fdb7b03a2bf9cad38c9e5687867a2fa2968120586f3bf2e6101c24cbe.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.005}=2.576:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, the $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100\\pm 2.576\\cdot \\frac{12}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ 100\\pm 9.775\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498573 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059825 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b90997d0-b563-5541-9fe2-36d7457da7b5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals From Normal Populations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16aed665-da6f-5199-b4a0-a23c3f6d73e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\pm 1.12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497852} {:answer/id #uuid "279474e9-ef4e-5abc-8ab4-8d1f3bd11164" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.3\\pm 1.69" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497880} {:answer/id #uuid "63adfc45-4a04-5e67-9643-aa70578c5133" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25\\pm 1.45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498037} {:answer/id #uuid "cb0597e7-4186-5021-b1a9-df1e48a1d95c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\pm 1.45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498252} {:answer/id #uuid "f81d09c4-1c72-5085-8688-6e7634892bfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\pm 1.69" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498340}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f81d09c4-1c72-5085-8688-6e7634892bfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5\\pm 1.69" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498340} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b4c8931-ac30-564c-ba77-710890c7c3aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498429}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b68ac86a-b851-54a2-a0bc-3666201fc505" :question/math-academy-id "q-142969" :question/problem "![](images/28/28f56656e191e342a4815ba74bdd29cba5247fba7d8ab0feca8ffbe0b785ab8c.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=25$ from a normal population with standard deviation $\\sigma =4.3.$ Given that the mean of the sample is $\\overset{―}{x}=-5,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" :question/worked-solution "We are told that the population is normally distributed. Therefore, the distribution of sample mean is also normal, and we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.025}=1.96.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -5\\pm 1.96\\cdot \\frac{4.3}{\\sqrt{25}} \\\\  & -5\\pm 1.69\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498508 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059825 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b90997d0-b563-5541-9fe2-36d7457da7b5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals From Normal Populations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58f93396-bb63-5e2d-9be2-5aff06d1100c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\pm 1.899" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498009} {:answer/id #uuid "668c3880-6cf7-5f86-8d9d-c48f288c2489" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\pm 2.193" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498043} {:answer/id #uuid "8299b9d2-aeff-5a18-a5df-fba1e994818a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\pm 1.899" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498103} {:answer/id #uuid "b0947152-9db1-51dc-96a4-f6cccad66ada" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\pm 0.731" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498192} {:answer/id #uuid "d3cba11e-5f53-5e85-aa22-88f50ef82d60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\pm 2.193" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498275}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58f93396-bb63-5e2d-9be2-5aff06d1100c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\pm 1.899" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498009} :answer-field/id #uuid "f02c5f87-b3cc-5c21-82fd-c00777a3a5e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498479}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2b3720b9-719b-5e5b-8310-cbdf4c3bbfe3" :question/math-academy-id "q-142973" :question/problem "![](images/39/39a83b1081d22919c9549c4ff1c6ad8006dc802cc23f9802d762e97883bea48c.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=12$ from a normal population with standard deviation $\\sigma =4.$ Given that the mean of the sample is $\\overset{―}{x}=9,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" :question/worked-solution "We are told that the population is normally distributed. Therefore, the distribution of sample mean is also normal, and we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.05}=1.645.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{12}} \\\\ 9\\pm 1.899\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498501 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059825 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b90997d0-b563-5541-9fe2-36d7457da7b5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals From Normal Populations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23f3c558-8e9a-5a12-bf1d-0fa1df352593" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-67\\pm 9.246" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497874} {:answer/id #uuid "510dac73-7b74-5411-b745-977232bca39f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\pm 6.028" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497990} {:answer/id 