\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.05}=1.645.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-67\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\frac{18}{\\sqrt{32}} \\\\ -67\\pm 5.234\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498562 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059825 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b90997d0-b563-5541-9fe2-36d7457da7b5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals From Normal Populations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "073bae64-2e98-5ef4-93fb-8f4f8dd2d449" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\pm 0.368" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497816} {:answer/id #uuid "54102a1f-c1ad-5e3f-a906-bb9bbb6db2f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\pm 0.638" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497997} {:answer/id #uuid "ac3e42f2-1f69-520f-b820-f536cfc81963" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\pm 0.635" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498180} {:answer/id #uuid "c74fb932-d6ff-5c7b-9eb5-ae431e835bc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\pm 0.583" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498241} {:answer/id #uuid "d485bb73-7ff3-505e-bcd7-133ad55e95a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\