>2.576)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.005}=2.576.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 12\\pm 2.576\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{49}} \\\\  & 12\\pm 0.368\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498543 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059824 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "27d20d28-3fba-5830-8bff-76e9a1c24bec" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals: Applications"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ef1c593-b4c7-5222-b654-ac60ee16ce57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1\\,950\\pm 528$ dollars" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497898} {:answer/id #uuid "4dc705e2-76e5-5216-86a9-dd85770b73a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1\\,950\\pm 71$ dollars" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497984} {:answer/id #uuid "9442daeb-08f3-5fbd-8243-a602d877dc23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1\\,950\\pm 373$ dollars" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498135} {:answer/id #uuid "a31c3860-8bc1-5373-9bd3-cf961c516f73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1\\,950\\pm 584$ dollars" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498163} {:answer/id #uuid "ad0fb17a-427f-52ae-a47b-05969843f0f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1\\,950\\pm 415$ dollars" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498184}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9442daeb-08f3-5fbd-8243-a602d877dc23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1\\,950\\pm 373$ dollars" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498135} :answer-field/id #uuid "32c160c3-a273-5a41-b70a-aa88ce3a1e92" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498382}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "66172e40-7344-58fd-a3d5-83fcbd16bd0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-143021" :question/problem "The daily income of a supermarket is normally distributed with standard deviation $\\sigma =1\\,200$ dollars. During a particular $28$-working-day month, the mean daily income was $1\\,950$ dollars. Find a $90%$ confidence interval for the mean daily income $\\mu $ of every work day.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" :question/worked-solution "We are told that the population is normally distributed. Therefore, the distribution of sample mean is also normal, and we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=1\\,950,\\,n=28,\\,\\sigma =1\\,200\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.05}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.05})=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/2f/2fc6327df99c6b88508b0c4de2e4edf6c5521974f05e2e2f02d09786937285a7.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.05}=1.645:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.05$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, the $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1\\,950\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\frac{1\\,200}{\\sqrt{28}} \\\\ 1\\,950\\pm 373\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498557 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059824 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "27d20d28-3fba-5830-8bff-76e9a1c24bec" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals: Applications"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "155ad1bd-d997-50f4-ab96-9007afb194f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.75\\pm 0.13\\,s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497848} {:answer/id #uuid "327a0039-b06e-53dd-bfc8-383328623244" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.55\\pm 0.03\\,s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497906} {:answer/id #uuid "8e3795ed-bdaf-5738-93ed-2a1185daa06c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2\\pm 0.08\\,s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498126} {:answer/id #uuid "cac0a256-72d4-5ce5-8410-231dbe55c448" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.55\\pm 0.08\\,s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498251} {:answer/id #uuid "cbfcdb9f-6c30-5b43-b4de-181ea4940af1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2\\pm 0.03\\,s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498258}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cac0a256-72d4-5ce5-8410-231dbe55c448" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.55\\pm 0.08\\,s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498251} :answer-field/id #uuid "74b47122-e0ef-5f16-b996-8efddca3bab2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498413}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "841adb19-e83e-5b6e-be60-db36b3558aba" :question/math-academy-id "q-143022" :question/problem "In order to measure the acceleration due to gravity, Annie knocks a ball off a table and records the time it takes to reach the ground. The measurements of the falling time of the ball are normally distributed with standard deviation $\\sigma =0.2\\,s.$ She repeats the experiment $16$ times and obtains a mean falling time of $0.55\\,s.$ Find a $90%$ confidence interval for the mean falling time $\\mu .$\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" :question/worked-solution "We are told that the population is normally distributed. Therefore, the distribution of sample mean is also normal, and we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=0.55\\,s,\\,n=16,\\,\\sigma =0.2\\,s\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.05}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.05})=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/2f/2fc6327df99c6b88508b0c4de2e4edf6c5521974f05e2e2f02d09786937285a7.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.05}=1.645:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.05$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, the $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.55\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\frac{0.2}{\\sqrt{16}} \\\\ 0.55\\pm 0.08\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498524 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059824 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "27d20d28-3fba-5830-8bff-76e9a1c24bec" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals: Applications"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44c8f4e3-9a6e-50ab-a851-2ca0685d61a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49.5\\pm 4.12\\,cm" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497953} {:answer/id #uuid "4593f573-da01-5e58-91b2-b20a7f84bb52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49.5\\pm 1.22\\,cm" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497955} {:answer/id #uuid "5b2069e1-e324-5627-8cfa-51a17d78e4d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49.5\\pm 3.14\\,cm" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498014} {:answer/id #uuid "75a879ce-e38a-59c6-9cde-2e3f1b82a578" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49.5\\pm 1.61\\,cm" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498079} {:answer/id #uuid "fc5b7218-6c68-5e18-b2b9-35212faa3729" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49.5\\pm 1.64\\,cm" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498348}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75a879ce-e38a-59c6-9cde-2e3f1b82a578" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49.5\\pm 1.61\\,cm" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498079} :answer-field/id #uuid "6bd35d8c-117b-596a-9cbd-63108e62ec80" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498411}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "40cf6769-0da8-5620-85fa-c881e7916a83" :question/math-academy-id "q-143080" :question/problem "The length, in centimeters, of newborn children in a particular hospital is distributed with a population standard deviation of $\\sigma =5\\,cm.$ On a particular day in a nursery room of the hospital, there are $64$ newborn children, and their mean length is $49.5\\,cm.$ Find a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean length $\\mu $ of the children born in that hospital.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size $n=64\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=49.5\\,cm,\\,n=64,\\,\\sigma =5\\,cm.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.005}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.005})=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/8c/8c5ac93fdb7b03a2bf9cad38c9e5687867a2fa2968120586f3bf2e6101c24cbe.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.005}=2.576:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, the $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}49.5\\pm 2.576\\cdot \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{64}} \\\\ 49.5\\pm 1.61\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498576 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059824 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "27d20d28-3fba-5830-8bff-76e9a1c24bec" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Confidence Intervals: Applications"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e4a964b-89d6-50b3-b070-923f25f24cda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\pm 0.470\\,hl" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497832} {:answer/id #uuid "15cb3f12-38ac-5ae3-b103-cecbd2328a68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\pm 3.527\\,hl" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497850} {:answer/id #uuid "45c91c8a-9fcd-528c-80ed-69a7d1d14cc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\pm 0.008\\,hl" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497957} {:answer/id #uuid "64fc6af7-ef67-5b4b-80fc-566d1be773ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\pm 2.684\\,hl" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498040} {:answer/id #uuid "a6df01ca-23cd-5023-bc1b-30fd13a5143d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\pm 0.358\\,hl" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498170}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e4a964b-89d6-50b3-b070-923f25f24cda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\pm 0.470\\,hl" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497832} :answer-field/id #uuid "02a05532-9f78-5d65-a4d0-0f27f0b07a9f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498362}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bb5fa69f-9c36-5007-b11d-0c6100a39ac6" :question/math-academy-id "q-143123" :question/problem "The amount of wine, in hectoliters, produced by one hectare of a vineyard in a particular region is distributed with a population standard deviation of $\\sigma =2\\,hl.$ A sample of $120$ hectares is examined, and it is found that the mean volume of wine produced by each hectare is $15\\,hl.$\nFind a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean hectoliters per hectare $\\mu $ of the entire region.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" :question/worked-solution "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size $n=120\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=15\\,hl,\\,n=120,\\,\\sigma =2\\,hl.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.005}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.005})=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/8c/8c5ac93fdb7b03a2bf9cad38c9e5687867a2fa2968120586f3bf2e6101c24cbe.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.005}=2.576:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, the $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15\\pm 2.576\\cdot \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{120}} \\\\ 15\\pm 0.470\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
